  "notes": "The kanji {観|み}る specifically refers to watching as a spectator — viewing performances, shows, films, and spectacles. While {見|み}る is the general-purpose \"to see/look at,\" {観|み}る implies appreciating or taking in something meant to be watched.\n\nWRITING:\n- {見|み}る: general seeing, looking at anything\n- {観|み}る: watching performances, shows, movies, spectacles\n- {診|み}る: examining (medical)\n- {視|み}る: inspecting, observing (formal)\n\nIn casual writing, {見|み}る or hiragana みる is often used for all meanings. {観|み}る is preferred in more careful or literary writing when referring to entertainment.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {映画|えいが}を{観|み}る: to watch a movie\n- {芝居|しばい}を{観|み}る: to watch a play\n- {試合|しあい}を{観|み}る: to watch a game\n- お{花見|はなみ}: flower viewing (uses the same {観|み} concept)\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {見|み}る: to see, to look at — the general-purpose verb; covers all types of seeing\n- {鑑賞|かんしょう}する: to appreciate (art, music) — more formal; emphasizes aesthetic appreciation\n- {観覧|かんらん}する: to view, to watch — formal; used for exhibitions and public events",
